{:edb/db-before-t 1979 :edb/db-after-t 1980 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535292 50 #inst "2026-10-08T21:25:57.610Z" 13194139535292 true] [17592186175863 1020 #edb/ref 17592186342186 13194139535292 true] [17592186175863 1020 #edb/ref 17592186342191 13194139535292 true] [17592186176018 1020 #edb/ref 17592186342164 13194139535292 true] [17592186176018 1020 #edb/ref 17592186342176 13194139535292 true] [17592186176018 1020 #edb/ref 17592186342180 13194139535292 true] [17592186176018 1020 #edb/ref 17592186342183 13194139535292 true] [17592186176021 1020 #edb/ref 17592186342171 13194139535292 true] [17592186176021 1020 #edb/ref 17592186342196 13194139535292 true] [17592186176021 1020 #edb/ref 17592186342202 13194139535292 true] [17592186176417 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535292 true] [17592186176417 1100 "The displacement $s$, in meters, relative to a fixed origin $O$ of a particle moving in a straight line along the $x$-axis is given by $s(t) = (3t - 5)^{2}$, where $t > 0$ is the time measured in seconds. Determine the time intervals in which the particle is moving in the direction of decreasing $x$." 13194139535292 false] [17592186176417 1100 "The displacement $s,$ in meters, relative to a fixed origin $O$ of a particle moving in a straight line along the $x$-axis is given by $s(t)=(3t-5)^{2}$, where $t>0$ is the time measured in seconds. Determine the time intervals in which the particle is moving in the direction of decreasing $x.$" 13194139535292 true] [17592186176417 1145 "If the particle moves in the direction of decreasing $x,$ then its velocity is negative. \n\nFirst, we calculate the velocity function by differentiating the position function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}((3t-5)^{2}) \\\\  & =6(3t-5)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo calculate time intervals in which the particle is moving in the direction of decreasing $x$, we solve the inequality $v(t)<0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & <0 \\\\ 6(3t-5) & <0 \\\\ 3t-5 & <0 \\\\ 3t & <5 \\\\ t & <\\frac{5}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the time interval in which the particle is moving to the direction of decreasing $x$ is $t\\in (0,\\frac{5}{3}).$" 13194139535292 true] [17592186261490 1017 #edb/ref 17592186274965 13194139535292 false] [17592186261490 1017 #edb/ref 17592186342208 13194139535292 true] [17592186342164 1017 #edb/ref 17592186342165 13194139535292 true] [17592186342164 1089 "q-109279" 13194139535292 true] [17592186342164 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535292 true] [17592186342164 1100 "The position $s(t)$ of a particle (in meters) at time $t$ seconds is given by \n\n$$\ns(t)={ln}^{2}⁡t+2,\\,t>0.\n$$\n\nCalculate the time at which the particle is stationary." 13194139535292 true] [17592186342164 1113 #uuid "a2aa3e69-2969-5499-b4e6-a1c16d2ad5a3" 13194139535292 true] [17592186342164 1145 "The particle is stationary when its velocity is zero. So first, we need to calculate the velocity function. We can do that by taking the derivative of the position function, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({ln}^{2}⁡t+2) \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡t}{t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe particle is stationary when $v=0\\,\\text{m/s}.$ So, we set $v=0$ and solve for $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡t}{t} & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡t & =0 \\\\ t & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particle is stationary at $t=1\\,\\text{s}.$" 13194139535292 true] [17592186342165 1000 #edb/ref 17592186342166 13194139535292 true] [17592186342165 1000 #edb/ref 17592186342167 13194139535292 true] [17592186342165 1000 #edb/ref 17592186342168 13194139535292 true] [17592186342165 1000 #edb/ref 17592186342169 13194139535292 true] [17592186342165 1000 #edb/ref 17592186342170 13194139535292 true] [17592186342165 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535292 true] [17592186342165 1041 "selection" 13194139535292 true] [17592186342165 1104 #edb/ref 17592186342167 13194139535292 true] [17592186342165 1110 #uuid "bae1f563-1213-5317-ba01-40ee04d62d05" 13194139535292 true] [17592186342166 1034 "t=\\frac{1}{e}\\,\\text{s}" 13194139535292 true] [17592186342166 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535292 true] [17592186342166 1111 #uuid "de14590c-5cb1-55d7-a957-286f17bd17e0" 13194139535292 true] [17592186342167 1034 "t=1\\,\\text{s}" 13194139535292 true] [17592186342167 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535292 true] [17592186342167 1111 #uuid "a9fe76ac-5201-5635-a26d-139134840c5f" 13194139535292 true] [17592186342168 1034 "t=e\\,\\text{s}" 13194139535292 true] [17592186342168 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535292 true] [17592186342168 1111 #uuid "eb5b4cd9-b88c-59aa-9d54-b8c464a4e2ae" 13194139535292 true] [17592186342169 1034 "t=2\\,\\text{s}" 13194139535292 true] [17592186342169 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535292 true] [17592186342169 1111 #uuid "6d472fbb-2846-5a9b-b80c-7f19d79a6a4b" 13194139535292 true] [17592186342170 1034 "t=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{e}}\\,\\text{s}" 13194139535292 true] [17592186342170 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535292 true] [17592186342170 1111 #uuid "e23b69c1-b7ec-544c-9288-68b145b0b595" 13194139535292 true] [17592186342171 1017 #edb/ref 17592186342172 13194139535292 true] [17592186342171 1017 #edb/ref 17592186342174 13194139535292 true] [17592186342171 1089 "q-315553" 13194139535292 true] [17592186342171 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139535292 true] [17592186342171 1100 "A particle $P$ is traveling along the $x$-axis with displacement $s(t)$ from a fixed origin $O$ given by\n\n$$\ns(t)=(5t-15){e}^{t},\\,t\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nFind the time at which the acceleration is zero, and find the value of the velocity at this moment.\n\nThe acceleration is zero at $t=$\n{{field-1}}, and the velocity at this moment is\n$v=$\n{{field-2}}." 13194139535292 true] [17592186342171 1113 #uuid "dd05cb61-1025-52d9-8f80-565c0086f261" 13194139535292 true] [17592186342171 1145 "First, we calculate $v(t)$ by differentiating $s(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}((5t-15){e}^{t}) \\\\  & =5{e}^{t}+(5t-15){e}^{t} \\\\  & =(5t-10){e}^{t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate $v(t)$ to find $a(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}((5t-10){e}^{t}) \\\\  & =5{e}^{t}+(5t-10){e}^{t} \\\\  & =(5t-5){e}^{t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we put $a=0$ to find the time when the acceleration is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(5t-5){e}^{t} & =0 \\\\ 5t-5 & =0 \\\\ t & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the acceleration is zero when $t=1.$ To find the velocity at this moment, we substitute this back into $v(t)$ to get\n\n$$\nv(1)=(5\\cdot 1-10){e}^{1}=-5e.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the acceleration is zero at $t=1,$ and the velocity at this moment is $v=-5e.$" 13194139535292 true] [17592186342172 1000 #edb/ref 17592186342173 13194139535292 true] [17592186342172 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535292 true] [17592186342172 1041 "field-1" 13194139535292 true] [17592186342172 1104 #edb/ref 17592186342173 13194139535292 true] [17592186342172 1110 #uuid "9d549997-b084-581c-bda3-216acfa1d1be" 13194139535292 true] [17592186342173 1034 "1" 13194139535292 true] [17592186342173 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535292 true] [17592186342173 1111 #uuid "c1910bb6-f3cf-5171-b2b6-2daa5437a3cc" 13194139535292 true] [17592186342174 1000 #edb/ref 17592186342175 13194139535292 true] [17592186342174 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535292 true] [17592186342174 1041 "field-2" 13194139535292 true] [17592186342174 1104 #edb/ref 17592186342175 13194139535292 true] [17592186342174 1110 #uuid "bd9cab1d-818b-5061-bc3b-f16d37e13237" 13194139535292 true] [17592186342175 1034 "-5e" 13194139535292 true] [17592186342175 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535292 true] [17592186342175 1111 #uuid "3c2b4319-bc98-5712-ad7c-66778fb352f2" 13194139535292 true] [17592186342176 1017 #edb/ref 17592186342177 13194139535292 true] [17592186342176 1089 "q-335684" 13194139535292 true] [17592186342176 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535292 true] [17592186342176 1100 "The position $s(t)$ of a particle (in meters) at time $t$ seconds is given by \n\n$$\ns(t)=\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}-7t+6},\\,t\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nCalculate the time at which the particle is stationary. Do not round your answer.\n\nTherefore, the particle is stationary at $t=$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\text{s}.$" 13194139535292 true] [17592186342176 1113 #uuid "d8ef2406-47cb-5949-bfc7-3df8354686c9" 13194139535292 true] [17592186342176 1145 "The particle is stationary when its velocity is zero. So first, we need to calculate the velocity function. We can do that by taking the derivative of the position function, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}-7t+6}) \\\\  & =\\frac{8t-7}{2\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}-7t+6}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe particle is stationary when $v=0\\,\\text{m/s}.$ So, we set $v=0$ and solve for $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{8t-7}{2\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}-7t+6}} & =0 \\\\ 8t-7 & =0 \\\\ t & =\\frac{7}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particle is stationary at $t=\\frac{7}{8}\\,\\text{s}.$" 13194139535292 true] [17592186342177 1000 #edb/ref 17592186342178 13194139535292 true] [17592186342177 1000 #edb/ref 17592186342179 13194139535292 true] [17592186342177 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535292 true] [17592186342177 1041 "field-1" 13194139535292 true] [17592186342177 1104 #edb/ref 17592186342178 13194139535292 true] [17592186342177 1110 #uuid "a3861490-cd42-533c-a625-98fe523eb3ff" 13194139535292 true] [17592186342178 1034 "\\frac{7}{8}" 13194139535292 true] [17592186342178 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535292 true] [17592186342178 1111 #uuid "b701c2c8-bb36-5117-92ac-ed5e9ae1e4c1" 13194139535292 true] [17592186342179 1034 "1" 13194139535292 true] [17592186342179 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535292 true] [17592186342179 1111 #uuid "588f6fb1-a586-5def-97d8-bf3fabd6137d" 13194139535292 true] [17592186342180 1017 #edb/ref 17592186342181 13194139535292 true] [17592186342180 1089 "q-335687" 13194139535292 true] [17592186342180 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535292 true] [17592186342180 1100 "The position $x(t)$ of a particle (in meters) at time $t$ seconds is given by \n\n$$\nx(t)=2{t}^{2}-10t+3,\\,t\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nCalculate the time at which the particle is stationary. Do not round your answer.\n\nTherefore, the particle is stationary at $t=$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\text{s}.$" 13194139535292 true] [17592186342180 1113 #uuid "476287a9-db73-54d5-ae8c-35bc0889d0f7" 13194139535292 true] [17592186342180 1145 "The particle is stationary when its velocity is zero. So first, we need to calculate the velocity function. We can do that by taking the derivative of the position function, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{t}^{2}-10t+3) \\\\  & =4t-10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe particle is stationary when $v=0\\,\\text{m/s}.$ So, we set $v=0$ and solve for $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4t-10 & =0 \\\\ 4t & =10 \\\\ t & =\\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particle is stationary at $t=\\frac{5}{2}\\,\\text{s}.$" 13194139535292 true] [17592186342181 1000 #edb/ref 17592186342182 13194139535292 true] [17592186342181 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535292 true] [17592186342181 1041 "field-1" 13194139535292 true] [17592186342181 1104 #edb/ref 17592186342182 13194139535292 true] [17592186342181 1110 #uuid "7d12f94d-a237-5802-87b3-e74c6daf96ee" 13194139535292 true] [17592186342182 1034 "\\frac{5}{2}" 13194139535292 true] [17592186342182 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535292 true] [17592186342182 1111 #uuid "d58b7edb-43ad-5310-96a1-f4d5e82fc075" 13194139535292 true] [17592186342183 1017 #edb/ref 17592186342184 13194139535292 true] [17592186342183 1089 "q-335696" 13194139535292 true] [17592186342183 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535292 true] [17592186342183 1100 "The position $x(t)$ of a particle (in meters) at time $t$ seconds is given by \n\n$$\nx(t)=t\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t),\\,t>0.\n$$\n\nCalculate the time at which the particle is stationary. Do not round your answer.\n\nTherefore, the particle is stationary at $t=$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\text{s}.$" 13194139535292 true] [17592186342183 1113 #uuid "a20eefee-8104-50ec-9236-d64413e98bff" 13194139535292 true] [17592186342183 1145 "The particle is stationary when its velocity is zero. So first, we need to calculate the velocity function. We can do that by taking the derivative of the position function, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t)) \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡(t)+t\\cdot \\frac{1}{t} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t)+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe particle is stationary when $v=0\\,\\text{m/s}.$ So, we set $v=0$ and solve for $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t)+1 & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(t) & =-1 \\\\ t & ={e}^{-1} \\\\ t & =\\frac{1}{e}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particle is stationary at $t=\\frac{1}{e}\\,\\text{s}.$" 13194139535292 true] [17592186342184 1000 #edb/ref 17592186342185 13194139535292 true] [17592186342184 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535292 true] [17592186342184 1041 "field-1" 13194139535292 true] [17592186342184 1104 #edb/ref 17592186342185 13194139535292 true] [17592186342184 1110 #uuid "6cce752b-5ee1-5083-a80a-796649da076b" 13194139535292 true] [17592186342185 1034 "\\frac{1}{e}" 13194139535292 true] [17592186342185 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535292 true] [17592186342185 1111 #uuid "132f0e53-8a2c-5262-8eeb-c3e9fe48f0ae" 13194139535292 true] [17592186342186 1017 #edb/ref 17592186342187 13194139535292 true] [17592186342186 1017 #edb/ref 17592186342189 13194139535292 true] [17592186342186 1089 "q-335713" 13194139535292 true] [17592186342186 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535292 true] [17592186342186 1100 "The displacement $s,$ in meters, relative to a fixed origin $O$ of a particle moving in a straight line along the $x$-axis is given by $s(t)=3t{e}^{-2t},$ where $t>0$ is the time, measured in seconds. Find the time intervals in which the particle is moving in the direction of decreasing $x.$\n\n$t\\in (${{field-1}},\n\n{{field-2}}$)$" 13194139535292 true] [17592186342186 1113 #uuid "7d78f851-53b4-5dbc-b66a-4ce2e2f325b9" 13194139535292 true] [17592186342186 1145 "If the particle moves in the direction of decreasing $x,$ then its velocity is negative. \n\nFirst, we calculate the velocity function by differentiating the position function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3t{e}^{-2t}) \\\\  & =3{e}^{-2t}+3t(-2){e}^{-2t} \\\\  & =(3-6t){e}^{-2t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo calculate time intervals in which the particle is moving in the direction of decreasing $x$, we solve $v(t)<0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & <0 \\\\ (3-6t){e}^{-2t} & <0 \\\\ 3-6t & <0 \\\\ -6t & <-3 \\\\ t & >\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the time interval in which the particle is moving to the direction of decreasing $x$ is \n\n$$\nt\\in (\\frac{1}{2},\\infty ).\n$$" 13194139535292 true] [17592186342187 1000 #edb/ref 17592186342188 13194139535292 true] [17592186342187 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535292 true] [17592186342187 1041 "field-1" 13194139535292 true] [17592186342187 1104 #edb/ref 17592186342188 13194139535292 true] [17592186342187 1110 #uuid "9d47248a-4800-5765-9ddb-343d2d3f6d68" 13194139535292 true] [17592186342188 1034 "\\frac{1}{2}" 13194139535292 true] [17592186342188 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535292 true] [17592186342188 1111 #uuid "6cc8197e-8bdc-56a0-9784-3ca0da57f8ff" 13194139535292 true] [17592186342189 1000 #edb/ref 17592186342190 13194139535292 true] [17592186342189 1040 #edb/ref 1759218604